---

<Base title={title}>
  <main id="main">
    <Header
      title={name}
      subtitle={tagline}
      contact={contact}
      nav={[
        { id: "web-development", label: "Web Development" },
        { id: "programming", label: "Programming" },
        { id: "the-minimalist", label: "The Minimalist" },
        { id: "technical-writing", label: "Technical Writing" },
      ]}
    >
      <p>AI has drastically increased productivity in software, but constant design and code generation comes with a cost: past certain point humans become unable to understand how something works.</p>

      <p><em>My job is to control complexity and make things understandable for the human mind.</em></p>

      <Figure slot="figure" src={coverImage} alt="Drawing of my electronics lab" ratio="3 / 2" eager class="lab">
        My lab, where I build and explore physical systems. <br>To know how things should work, <br>first you have to understand the world.
      </Figure>
    </Header>



    <Section id="the-minimalist" title="The Minimalist" flush>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="col-6">

          <p>A website should provide information clearly. An application should do its job and get out of the way. A novel told in letters should read like letters. Everything must have a purpose, and have the integrity to stick to it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="col-6">
          <p>My goal is to reduce things to their essential qualities and making their meaning clear and legible—Removing anything that doesn’t belong, but preserve and make the essentials stand out.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Subsection id="lens" title="Lens">
        <div class="grid">
          <div class="col-6">
            <Figure
              src={safariExtensionImage}
              alt="The CTAN page for the microtype package, restyled by the Minimalist Lens extension"
              ratio="8 / 5"
              class="offset"
            >
              See the <a href="https://ctan.org/pkg/microtype" target="_blank">original page</a> for the example shown in this picture.
            </Figure>
          </div>

          <div class="col-6">
            <p>An experimental Safari extension written in Swift and JavaScript that modifies the design of content-based websites: typography-oriented, no ornamentation; only what’s necessary to make the content the main subject and keep it as legible as possible.</p>

            <p>Currently on hold to adopt The Minimalist brand. Chrome port in progress.</p>

            <Links links={[{ label: "See on the App Store", url: "https://apps.apple.com/us/app/minimalist-design/id6765779070?mt=12" }]} />
          </div>
        </div>
      </Subsection>

      <Subsection id="books" title="Press">
        <div class="grid">
          <div class="col-6">
            <p>A collection of well-typeset editions of public domain books. Their purpose is to make reading more enjoyable, to retain attention, and to reflect the tone of the stories through their design.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="col-6">
            <p>These books are published as PDF files to maintain a fixed layout and make sure everything remains organized and aligned with precision. Ideal for tablets and larger screens.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <BookList books={books} />
      </Subsection>
    </Section>

    <Section id="programming" title="Programming">
      <div class="grid">
        <p class="col-6">The art of applied abstraction.</p>
      </div>

      <Subsection id="emacs" title="Emacs">
        <div class="grid">
          <div class="col-6">
            <Figure src={emacsImage} alt="Emacs Lisp code in my Emacs configuration" ratio="8 / 5" class="framed" />
          </div>

          <div class="col-6">
            <p>An Emacs configuration built for clear reading and closer attention to code semantics, by omitting high-contrast coloring of all syntax. There is only high contrast between comments and code, to make emphasis in documentation.</p>

            <p>I use it exclusively on the terminal because I find that pure text provides me with a better editing experience, without visual artifacts. And also I can edit files remotely in the same way I do locally, which I do quite often.</p>

            <p>The environment that Emacs provides for live and interactive programming is unmatched. That has also created a strong interest for languages like Scheme and Forth.</p>

            <Links links={[{ label: "See on GitHub", url: "https://github.com/objectiveprover/.emacs.d" }]} />
          </div>
        </div>
      </Subsection>
    </Section>

    <Section id="concise" title="Concise CSS">
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="col-6">
          <Figure src={conciseImage} alt="The Concise CSS documentation website" ratio="3 / 4" class="framed" />
        </div>

        <div class="col-6">
          <p>I started building my own CSS framework in 2014; then I found Concise CSS and noticed that Keenan Payne and I had the same vision for it. In 2015 we merged the projects and released it as a new, distinctive framework compared to the others available at the time: native HTML elements with a normalized design good enough to be used on their own, a collection of components with minimal design for easy customization, and utility styles for rapid prototyping or component variations.</p>

          <p>It included a grid system that was more intuitive because it used HTML attributes instead of classes, which was unusual at the time, before element attributes became the default way to attach data to components.</p>

          <p>I led the typography-oriented design for the website, following the principles of the framework; it was composed mostly of text and code samples. I wrote most of it, and Keenan helped with editing and grammar corrections (I didn’t have much experience with English yet).</p>

          <p>The next version grew more ambitious and included its own PostCSS-based toolchain, which let us define and auto-calculate custom units for font sizes and element spacing, standardizing design systems before CSS custom properties had broad browser support.</p>

          <p>Concise CSS was even included in some books about web development, including one by Larry Ullman, the first author I read about web development as a teenager.</p>

          <p>The project has been frozen for a couple of years, but a new version is still planned to unify all the existing components and use the latest native CSS features.</p>

          <Links
              links={[
                { label: "GitHub", url: "https://github.com/concisecss" },
                { label: "Website", url: "https://concisecss.com/" },
              ]}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="clients" title="Clients">
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="col-6">
          <p>My career as a web developer started in 2014 building websites for clients as a freelancer. Later on, I started working as a long-term contractor for more ambitious projects in collaboration with other developers and designers.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="col-6">
          <p>My goal from the beginning has been to build websites in the simplest and most effective way possible, ensuring maintainability and performance.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ClientList clients={clients} />

      <Note>
        This is only a partial list of the websites I have built since 2019. Some of these have since been redesigned or replaced. Earlier versions can probably be found on the <a href="https://web.archive.org/">Wayback Machine</a>.
      </Note>
    </Section>

    <Section id="technical-writing" title="Technical Writing">
      {articleGroups.map((g) => (
        <LinkGroup title={g.title} subtitle={g.subtitle} items={g.articles} newTab />
      ))}

      <LinkGroup
        title="Appearances in Books"
        subtitle="Publisher anthologies containing my articles."
        items={bookAppearances}
      />
    </Section>
  </main>

  <footer class="colophon">
    <p class="copyright">Copyright {year}, {name}</p>
    <a href={contact}>Contact</a>
  </footer>
</Base>
